- op: discuss  # ee63
  at: '2026-09-15T22:46:08Z'  # ee63
  by: agent  # ee63
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ee63
  clock: 6  # ee63
  nonce: ee63  # ee63
  message: "DONE in PR #977 (merged), which folded this in for the reason given above: the\nlock conversion rewrites the very lines this item is about, so keeping them apart\nwould have meant writing the known-wrong comment once more.\n\nVERIFIED ON THE MERGED TREE -- all five sites, by reading them back rather than\ntrusting the diff:\n\n  * scripts/auto-pr 1085 / 1494 / 2326: the three \"this action's git operations\n    (fetch + post-merge fast-forward)\" comments are gone. Those sites now call\n    _autopr_hold_recover_lock, whose header states the span is the WHOLE action\n    INCLUDING the CI poll, cites the 2400s default and 300s soft-retry, and says\n    outright that the old wording is how WI-pohir came to believe fifteen minutes\n    was a safe bound.\n  * .githooks/reference-transaction:35: was \"set this marker around their fetch +\n    fast-forward\" -- wrong for do_sync too, whose marker spans sync.py:1512 to\n    the finally at 2034 with a CI poll at 1706 in between. Now states the real\n    span and why the guard tests a lock.\n  * .githooks/reference-transaction:22: the clean/stash gap pointed at a manual\n    `tracker recover`. Now points at `tracker reconcile` and says explicitly that\n    `recover` is the deprecated journal-only half which leaves the repo behind\n    the upstream -- matching what AGENTS.md already told operators.\n\nThe non-governance half shipped separately in PR #975 as planned (cli.py and\ntest_reconcile.py, both saying \"around its own fetch\").\n\nWHY THIS ITEM EXISTED AT ALL, worth keeping on the record: WI-pohir's refuted\npremise was not invented. It was a faithful reading of these comments. A comment\nthat understates the extent of the thing it documents is a latent wrong premise\nfor every future reader, and this one had already produced a proposed fix that\nwould have false-alarmed on every auto-pr with slow CI."  # ee63
